\value{
\code{go_*()} functions return a graph object;
  \code{add_*()} functions return the modified figure.
}
\description{
The quiver trace type visualizes vector fields using arrows. Specify a
vector field using 4 1D arrays: 2 position arrays `x`, `y` and 2 vector
component arrays `u`, `v`. The arrows are drawn exactly at the positions
given by `x` and `y`. Arrow length and direction are determined by `u`
and `v` components.
}
\details{
\code{go_quiver()} creates a validated graph object (a named list).
\code{add_quiver()} creates one and adds it to a figure.


Nested objects are given as named lists or with their constructors, which
are linked from the arguments below. Every property is checked against
the plotly.js schema when the object is created or modified (see
\code{\link{plotly_obj-modify}}). One-sided formulas (\code{~col}) are
evaluated in the data, and then checked, when the trace is added with
\code{add_<trace>()} or \code{add_trace()}.
}
\examples{
d <- expand.grid(x = 1:5, y = 1:5)
d |>
  go_figure() |>
  add_quiver(x = ~x, y = ~y, u = ~y / 10, v = ~-x / 10)
}
\seealso{
\code{\link{go_figure}()}, \code{\link{add_trace}()}
}
